ARM: dts: omap5: add the operating points above the part's rating

The previous patch fills in the gaps below 1.5 GHz without raising the
ceiling.  This one raises it, to 2 GHz, and it is the half that can cost
you hardware rather than a reboot.

The OMAP5432 on this board is a 1.5 GHz-rated general purpose part.  Five
points above that rating:

	1600 MHz  1.300 V
	1700 MHz  1.350 V
	1800 MHz  1.350 V
	1900 MHz  1.350 V
	2000 MHz  1.450 V

The tree already carried a 1700 MHz point behind an unexplained `#if 0`
marked "potentially overclocking", asking for 1.25 V.  That is 100 mV
below what the same frequency is given here.  Whoever wrote the guarded
entry and whoever published the table disagree about the voltage that
frequency needs, and the higher figure is the one that comes with a
report of having been run.  Take the higher one and remove the stub, so
there is one answer in the file rather than two.

The supply is constrained to 1.5 V, so 1.45 V is inside the regulator's
range with 50 mV to spare.  That is the only claim in this patch that has
been checked against this board.

Everything else here is unverified on this unit.  The author of the table
says plainly that 2 GHz needs modified cooling and that they can hardly
run it themselves, and this machine already reaches 95 C under sustained
emulation with a 100 C passive trip, which leaves five degrees before it
throttles and no headroom at all for a higher clock.  Adding the points
does not by itself make the machine use them: pyra-thermal.sh caps the
maximum frequency and must be raised separately.  Leave it alone on the
first boot, so that the tree is tested before anything is overclocked.

This is an experiment and it is not part of the Pyra fix set.

--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/omap5-letux-cortex15-common.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/omap5-letux-cortex15-common.dtsi
@@ -95,9 +95,17 @@
 		1250000 1100000
 		1400000 1200000
 		1500000 1250000
-#if 0	/* potentially overclocking */
-		1700000 1250000
-#endif
+		/*
+		 * Above this line the part is past its rating.  The 1700000
+		 * entry that used to sit here under an #if 0 asked for
+		 * 1250000 uV; these ask for 1350000, which is the voltage
+		 * the steps were reported stable at on real hardware.
+		 */
+		1600000 1300000
+		1700000 1350000
+		1800000 1350000
+		1900000 1350000
+		2000000 1450000
 	>;
 };
 
